\title{Bounds for Induced-Universal Posets:\\ Counting, Overlap, and an Explicit Bipartite Host}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{7 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A poset $H$ is \emph{induced-universal} for the $n$-element posets if every partial order on $n$ points occurs as an induced subposet of $H$; write $U(n)$ for the least number of points of such a host. The classical principal-ideal embedding gives $U(n)\le 2^{n}$, and a recent line of work shows that for every $\eta>0$ and all sufficiently large $n$ there is a host of size $2^{(1+\eta)n/2}$, via a transitivity-preserving labelling scheme and the Szemer\'edi Regularity Lemma. We develop, from first principles, the quantitative landscape surrounding that theorem.

On the upper side we sharpen the naive bound to $U(n)\le 2^{n}-1$, so that the naive bound is never attained; and we construct an explicit \emph{tagged-neighbourhood host} on $k+2^{k}l$ points containing every $(k,l)$-bipartite poset as an induced subposet, attaining the exponent $n/2$ on the balanced bipartite family with no asymptotics and no regularity argument. We prove that the tag coordinate is necessary.

On the lower side we prove a counting bound $2^{kl}\le N^{k+l}$ for any host of the $(k,l)$-bipartite family, hence $N\ge 2^{kl/(k+l)}$ and $\log_{2}U(n)\ge (n-1)/4$ for all $n\ge 1$; we re-derive it through the comparability-graph functor, on the graph side, where the regularity lemma lives. We then develop a purely structural \emph{overlap method}: if two $n$-element posets share no common induced subposet on more than $s$ points, any host has at least $2n-s$ points. This yields $U(n)\ge 2n-1$ and, with a third poset, $U(n)\ge 3n-\lceil n/2\rceil-3\sim\tfrac{5}{2}n$. Finally, by playing a geometric family of chain-unions $C_{4^{0}},\dots,C_{4^{k-1}}$ against each other and applying a Bonferroni inequality for $k$ sets, we prove $2k\,4^{k}\le 3U(4^{k})$ and $n\log_{4}n\le 6U(n)$ for all $n$, so $U(n)/n\to\infty$; ratio $4$ is above the threshold ratio $2$ at which the method degenerates.

We also prove $U$ strictly increasing and determine $U(0)=0$, $U(1)=1$, $U(2)=3$, $U(3)=5$, $7\le U(4)\le 8$, the last by an explicit eight-point host verified against all $219$ four-element orders. The exact values show that the counting bound is lossy already at $n=2$.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} universal poset, induced subposet, adjacency labelling scheme, comparability graph, Szemer\'edi Regularity Lemma, Bonferroni inequality, extremal set theory.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}

Let $P=(X,\le_{P})$ and $H=(V,\le_{H})$ be posets. An \emph{induced embedding} of $P$ into $H$ is a map $f:X\to V$ with
\[
f(x)\le_{H}f(y)\iff x\le_{P}y\qquad\text{for all }x,y\in X.
\]
Such a map is automatically injective: if $f(x)=f(y)$ then reflexivity of $\le_{H}$ gives $f(x)\le_{H}f(y)$ and $f(y)\le_{H}f(x)$, hence $x\le_{P}y$ and $y\le_{P}x$, hence $x=y$ by antisymmetry. The image of $f$, with the order inherited from $H$, is a copy of $P$; we say $P$ is an \emph{induced subposet} of $H$.

\begin{definition}
$H$ is \emph{induced-universal for size $n$} if every partial order on an $n$-element set embeds into $H$ as an induced subposet. Define
\[
U(n)=\min\{\,|V| : (V,\le)\text{ is a finite poset, induced-universal for size }n\,\}.
\]
\end{definition}

The minimum is over a nonempty set of naturals (Theorem~\ref{thm:boolean} supplies a host), so it exists and is attained. The problem is to determine $U(n)$. It is the poset analogue of the induced-universal graph problem, and it is equivalent to a question about adjacency labelling schemes (Section~\ref{sec:graphs}).

\subsection{What is known, and what this paper proves}

The elementary upper bound $U(n)\le 2^{n}$ comes from the principal-ideal embedding into the Boolean lattice. The state of the art is that for every $\eta>0$ and all sufficiently large $n$ there is an induced-universal host of size $2^{(1+\eta)n/2}$; that construction proceeds by a labelling scheme designed so that transitivity is automatically preserved, and its central step invokes the Szemer\'edi Regularity Lemma. This paper is a self-contained development of the surrounding quantitative theory.

\subsection{Notation}

$[n]=\{0,1,\dots,n-1\}$. For a poset $P$ and $x\in P$, the \emph{principal down-set} is $\down x=\{y:y\le x\}$. A \emph{chain} is a totally ordered poset, an \emph{antichain} one with no relations besides equality. $\PP(S)$ is the power set of $S$, ordered by inclusion; $\lceil n/2\rceil$ denotes $\lfloor (n+1)/2\rfloor$.

\section{Upper bounds: the Boolean lattice and the ideal host}

\begin{lemma}[Ideals encode the order]\label{lem:ideal}
Let $P$ be a poset on $X$. For $x,y\in X$, $\ \down x\subseteq \down y\iff x\le y$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) If $x\le y$ and $z\le x$, then $z\le y$ by transitivity. ($\Rightarrow$) Reflexivity gives $x\in\down x\subseteq\down y$, i.e.\ $x\le y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Boolean host]\label{thm:boolean}
For every poset $P$ on $X$, the map $x\mapsto\down x$ is an induced embedding of $P$ into $\PP(X)$. In particular $\PP([n])$ is induced-universal with $2^{n}$ points, so $U(n)\le 2^{n}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:ideal} is exactly the induced-embedding condition; injectivity is automatic and $|\PP(X)|=2^{n}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Relation-level universality]
Theorem~\ref{thm:boolean} says more than ``every $n$-element poset embeds somewhere''. Fixing the ground set $X$ once and for all, \emph{every} partial order relation $r$ on $X$ is realised by the single recipe $x\mapsto\{y:r(y,x)\}$. It is convenient to say that an ordered set $V$ is a \emph{universal host for the ground set $X$} if for every partial order $r$ on $X$ there is $f:X\to V$ with $f(x)\le f(y)\iff r(x,y)$. This phrasing makes restriction to subclasses and transport along bijections of $X$ trivial.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Ideal host]\label{thm:idealhost}
Let $\NN_{n}$ be the poset of all nonempty subsets of $[n]$ ordered by inclusion. Then $\NN_{n}$ is induced-universal for size $n$ and $|\NN_{n}|=2^{n}-1$; hence $U(n)\le 2^{n}-1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By reflexivity $x\in\down x$, so every principal down-set is nonempty and the embedding of Theorem~\ref{thm:boolean} already lands in $\NN_{n}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$U(n)<2^{n}$ for every $n\ge0$: the naive bound is never attained.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Every nonempty label is used]\label{prop:labels}
For every nonempty $S\subseteq[n]$ and every $x\in S$ there is a partial order $r$ on $[n]$ with $\{y:r(y,x)\}=S$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Put $r(a,b)$ iff $a=b$, or ($a\in S$, $b=x$, $a\ne x$). Reflexivity is clear. A nontrivial composite would require $b=x$ and $b\in S$, $b\ne x$, impossible, so $r$ is transitive. If $a\ne b$ with $r(a,b)$ and $r(b,a)$ then $b=x=a$, so $r$ is antisymmetric. Finally $\{y:r(y,x)\}=\{x\}\cup(S\setminus\{x\})=S$.
\end{proof}

Proposition~\ref{prop:labels} does \emph{not} say $U(n)=2^{n}-1$; it says only that the down-set labelling itself cannot be trimmed. Indeed $U(3)=5<7=2^{3}-1$: the gain must come from a different labelling, which is exactly what the $2^{(1+\eta)n/2}$ theorem provides.

\section{The counting lower bound}

\begin{definition}
Let $R\subseteq[k]\times[l]$. The \emph{$(k,l)$-bipartite poset} $B(R)$ has ground set $[k]\sqcup[l]$ (elements $a_{i}$, $b_{j}$) with $a_{i}\le a_{i'}\iff i=i'$, $b_{j}\le b_{j'}\iff j=j'$, $a_{i}\le b_{j}\iff (i,j)\in R$, and $b_{j}\not\le a_{i}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:bip}
$B(R)$ is a partial order for every $R$, and $R\mapsto B(R)$ is injective; there are exactly $2^{kl}$ distinct $(k,l)$-bipartite posets.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Reflexivity is immediate. The only composable pair of non-equalities would be $a\le b_{j}$ and $b_{j}\le a'$, and the latter never holds, so transitivity reduces to equality substitution. Antisymmetry: $a_{i}\le b_{j}$ and $b_{j}\le a_{i}$ cannot both hold. Injectivity: $R$ is recovered as the set of pairs with $a_{i}\le b_{j}$.
\end{proof}

Call a host $H$ \emph{$(k,l)$-bipartite-universal} if every $B(R)$ embeds into $H$ as an induced subposet. Any host universal for the $(k+l)$-element posets is in particular $(k,l)$-bipartite-universal.

\begin{theorem}[Counting bound]\label{thm:count}
If $H$ is a $(k,l)$-bipartite-universal poset with $N$ points, then $2^{kl}\le N^{\,k+l}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For each $R$ choose an induced embedding $F_{R}:[k]\sqcup[l]\to H$. The map $R\mapsto F_{R}$ is injective: if $F_{R}=F_{S}$ then for all $i,j$,
\[
(i,j)\in R\iff F_{R}(a_{i})\le F_{R}(b_{j})\iff F_{S}(a_{i})\le F_{S}(b_{j})\iff (i,j)\in S .
\]
There are $2^{kl}$ relations and $N^{k+l}$ maps $[k]\sqcup[l]\to H$.
\end{proof}

The proof is an injection, not an entropy estimate: an induced copy \emph{is} a complete record of the poset it copies.

\begin{corollary}[Analytic form]
If $k+l>0$ and $H$ is nonempty, then $N\ge 2^{\,kl/(k+l)}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
With $x=kl/(k+l)$ we have $(2^{x})^{k+l}=2^{kl}\le N^{k+l}$; take $(k+l)$-th roots of nonnegative reals.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Balanced form]\label{cor:balanced}
If $m\ge1$ and $H$ is $(m,m)$-bipartite-universal with $N$ points, then $2^{m}\le N^{2}$; on $n=2m$ points, $N\ge 2^{n/4}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
If $N^{2}<2^{m}$ then $N^{2m}<2^{m^{2}}$, contradicting $2^{m\cdot m}\le N^{m+m}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:split}
For every $n$, $\ 2^{\lfloor n/2\rfloor\lceil n/2\rceil}\le U(n)^{\,n}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Split $[n]$ into parts of sizes $\lfloor n/2\rfloor$ and $\lceil n/2\rceil$; a universal host is $(\lfloor n/2\rfloor,\lceil n/2\rceil)$-bipartite-universal, and Theorem~\ref{thm:count} applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Logarithmic bounds]\label{thm:log}
For every $n\ge1$, $\ \dfrac{n-1}{4}\le\log_{2}U(n)\le n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The upper bound is Theorem~\ref{thm:boolean}. For the lower bound put $K=\lfloor n/2\rfloor\lceil n/2\rceil$; taking $\log_{2}$ in Lemma~\ref{lem:split} gives $K\le n\log_{2}U(n)$. A parity split gives $n(n-1)\le 4K$: for $n=2m$, $n(n-1)\le n^{2}=4m^{2}=4K$; for $n=2m+1$, $4K=4m(m+1)=n^{2}-1\ge n(n-1)$. Divide by $n>0$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Where the factor of two is lost}

Restricted to the balanced bipartite family on $n=2m$ points there are $2^{n^{2}/4}$ posets; a host on $N=2^{cn}$ points offers $N^{n}=2^{cn^{2}}$ maps, and the injection of Theorem~\ref{thm:count} is defeated only when $c<1/4$. It is therefore structurally impossible for pure counting to certify $c>1/4$: the method never observes that a single host point is reused by astronomically many embeddings. Quantifying that reuse is the role of the regularity method in the $2^{(1+\eta)n/2}$ theorem.

\section{An explicit host attaining exponent $n/2$ on the bipartite family}

\begin{definition}\label{def:biphost}
For $k,l\ge0$ let $B_{k,l}=[k]\sqcup(\PP([k])\times[l])$, ordered by: the elements of $[k]$ are pairwise incomparable; the pairs $(S,t)$ are pairwise incomparable; $a\le(S,t)\iff a\in S$; and $(S,t)\not\le a$. This is a partial order, and $|B_{k,l}|=k+2^{k}l$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Bipartite universality]\label{thm:bipuniv}
$B_{k,l}$ contains every $(k,l)$-bipartite poset as an induced subposet.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Given $R$, set $f(a_{i})=i$ and $f(b_{j})=(N_{R}(j),j)$ with $N_{R}(j)=\{i:(i,j)\in R\}$. Then $f(a_{i})\le f(a_{i'})$ iff $i=i'$; $f(a_{i})\le f(b_{j})$ iff $i\in N_{R}(j)$ iff $a_{i}\le b_{j}$; $f(b_{j})\not\le f(a_{i})$; and $f(b_{j})\le f(b_{j'})$ iff the pairs are equal, which by comparing second coordinates holds iff $j=j'$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Necessity of the tag]\label{prop:tag}
Let $H$ contain every $(k,2)$-bipartite poset as an induced subposet, and let $f$ be an induced embedding of $B(\varnothing)$. Then $f(b_{0})\ne f(b_{1})$, even though $b_{0}$ and $b_{1}$ have identical down-sets.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
If $f(b_{0})=f(b_{1})$ then $f(b_{0})\le f(b_{1})$, so $b_{0}\le b_{1}$ in $B(\varnothing)$, i.e.\ $0=1$.
\end{proof}

Thus a labelling by neighbourhoods alone is never injective when duplicates occur, and any correct scheme must carry a disambiguating coordinate. The tag costs a factor $l$ in the host size, i.e.\ $\log_{2}(n/2)$ extra bits per element on balanced parts --- negligible against the leading exponent.

\begin{corollary}[Balanced bipartite sandwich]\label{cor:sandwich}
Let $U_{\mathrm{bip}}(k,l)$ be the least size of a $(k,l)$-bipartite-universal poset. For every $m\ge1$,
\[
2^{\,m/2}\ \le\ U_{\mathrm{bip}}(m,m)\ \le\ m\,2^{m}+m .
\]
On $n=2m$ points: exponent at least $n/4$, at most $n/2+O(\log n)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Corollary~\ref{cor:balanced} and Theorem~\ref{thm:bipuniv} with $k=l=m$.
\end{proof}

So on the very subfamily generating the general lower bound, the exponent $n/2$ is attained by a construction one can write on a napkin. What the regularity-based argument buys is the extension of that exponent from height-$\le2$ posets to all posets, at the cost of the $\eta$.

\begin{theorem}[Sandwich for the full class]
For $n=2m$ with $m\ge1$: the poset $\PP([n])$ is induced-universal with $2^{n}$ points, and every induced-universal $H$ on $N$ points satisfies $2^{m}\le N^{2}$, i.e.\ $N\ge 2^{n/4}$. The $2^{(1+\eta)n/2}$ theorem interpolates between the two.
\end{theorem}

\section{The overlap method: structural lower bounds}

\begin{definition}
Let $P,Q$ be posets on $[n]$. Say $(P,Q)$ has \emph{common induced bound $s$} if every poset embedding as an induced subposet into both has at most $s$ points; concretely, whenever $A\subseteq[n]$ and $\varphi$ is injective on $A$ with $x\le_{P}y\iff\varphi(x)\le_{Q}\varphi(y)$ for all $x,y\in A$, we have $|A|\le s$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Overlap of two induced copies]\label{lem:overlap}
Let $f,g$ be induced embeddings of $P,Q$ into a poset $H$, where $(P,Q)$ has common induced bound $s$. Then $|f([n])\cap g([n])|\le s$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Let $A=\{x:f(x)\in g([n])\}$ and $\varphi=g^{-1}\circ f$ on $A$ ($g$ is injective). Then $\varphi$ is injective on $A$, and for $x,y\in A$,
\[
x\le_{P}y\iff f(x)\le_{H}f(y)\iff g(\varphi x)\le_{H}g(\varphi y)\iff \varphi x\le_{Q}\varphi y .
\]
So $|A|\le s$, and $|f([n])\cap g([n])|=|f(A)|=|A|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Overlap principle]\label{thm:overlap}
If a host $H$ on $N$ points contains induced copies of $n$-element posets $P,Q$ with common induced bound $s$, then $N\ge 2n-s$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$N\ge|A\cup B|=|A|+|B|-|A\cap B|\ge 2n-s$, with $A,B$ the two images.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:chainanti}
The $n$-chain and the $n$-antichain have common induced bound $1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
A common induced subposet is both totally ordered and totally unordered; two distinct points would be simultaneously comparable and incomparable.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:2n1}
$U(n)\ge 2n-1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}[Bonferroni for three sets]\label{lem:bonf3}
$|A\cup B\cup C|\ge|A|+|B|+|C|-|A\cap B|-|A\cap C|-|B\cap C|$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{definition}
Let $D_{n}$ be the poset on $[n]$ that is the disjoint union of the chain on $\{0,\dots,\lceil n/2\rceil-1\}$ and the chain on the rest: $x\le y$ iff $x\le y$ as integers and $x,y$ lie on the same side.
\end{definition}

Three overlap estimates: chain versus antichain has bound $1$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:chainanti}); chain versus $D_{n}$ has bound $\lceil n/2\rceil$, since a chain inside $D_{n}$ lies entirely on one side and each side has at most $\lceil n/2\rceil$ points; antichain versus $D_{n}$ has bound $2$, since an antichain meets each side in at most one point.

\begin{theorem}[Three-poset bound]\label{thm:three}
$U(n)\ge 3n-\bigl(3+\lceil n/2\rceil\bigr)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $A,B,C$ be the images of induced copies of the chain, the antichain and $D_{n}$ in an optimal host; each has $n$ points, and the pairwise intersections have at most $1$, $\lceil n/2\rceil$, $2$ points. Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:bonf3}.
\end{proof}

Asymptotically this is $\tfrac{5}{2}n$ and exceeds $2n-1$ for all $n\ge6$; at $n=4$ it gives exactly $7$.

\begin{theorem}[Strict monotonicity]\label{thm:mono}
$U(n)<U(n+1)$ for every $n$; in particular $U$ is strictly increasing and injective.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $H$ be an optimal host for size $n+1$, so $|H|=U(n+1)\ge n+1\ge1$, and fix a maximal point $m$ of $H$. Let $P$ be a poset on $n$ points and let $P^{+}$ adjoin a new top $\top$; embed $P^{+}$ into $H$ by $F$. For $x\in P$ we have $F(x)\le F(\top)$. If $F(x)=m$ then $m\le F(\top)$, so $F(\top)=m=F(x)$ by maximality, contradicting injectivity of $F$ since $x\ne\top$. Hence $F|_{P}$ is an induced embedding into $H\setminus\{m\}$, so $U(n)\le U(n+1)-1$.
\end{proof}

\section{Exact small values}

\begin{theorem}
$U(0)=0$ and $U(1)=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}
$U(2)=3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Upper: $B_{1,1}$ of Definition~\ref{def:biphost} has $1+2\cdot1=3$ points --- a bottom point $a$ and the pairs $(\varnothing,0)$, $(\{a\},0)$ --- and hosts both two-element posets: the antichain as $\{a,(\varnothing,0)\}$, the chain as $\{a,(\{a\},0)\}$. Lower: Corollary~\ref{cor:2n1}.
\end{proof}

The counting bound gives only $2^{1\cdot1}\le N^{2}$, i.e.\ $N\ge2$: \emph{it is already lossy at $n=2$.}

\begin{theorem}
$U(3)=5$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Lower: Corollary~\ref{cor:2n1}. Upper: an explicit five-point poset hosts all $19$ partial orders on three labelled points; exhibiting the embeddings is a finite verification over the $2^{9}$ candidate relations on three points, of which $19$ are partial orders.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
$7\le U(4)\le 8$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Lower: Corollary~\ref{cor:2n1} (or Theorem~\ref{thm:three}). Upper: an explicit eight-point host, with an embedding for each of the $219$ partial orders on four labelled points, found by a finite search over the $2^{12}$ candidate relations on four points.
\end{proof}

Whether $U(4)$ equals $7$ or $8$ is open here.

\section{Superlinearity: a geometric family of chain-unions}

\begin{definition}
For $d\ge1$, let $C_{n,d}$ be the poset on $[n]$ with $x\le y$ iff $x\le y$ as integers and $\lfloor x/d\rfloor=\lfloor y/d\rfloor$: cut $[n]$ into consecutive blocks of length $d$, make each block a chain, impose no relations across blocks. Thus $C_{n,1}$ is the antichain and $C_{n,n}$ the chain.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Chain-union overlap]\label{lem:chainunion}
For all $d,e\ge1$, the pair $(C_{n,e},C_{n,d})$ has common induced bound
\[
\Bigl(\Bigl\lfloor\frac{n-1}{e}\Bigr\rfloor+1\Bigr)\cdot d ,
\]
i.e.\ (number of blocks of the coarse poset) $\times$ (block size of the fine poset).
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Let $A$ and $\varphi$ realise a common induced subposet, and map $x\in A$ to $\psi(x)=(\lfloor x/e\rfloor,\ \varphi(x)\bmod d)$. The first coordinate takes at most $\lfloor(n-1)/e\rfloor+1$ values and the second at most $d$, so it suffices that $\psi$ be injective on $A$. If $\psi(x)=\psi(y)$, equal first coordinates put $x,y$ in the same block of $C_{n,e}$, hence they are comparable there, hence $\varphi(x),\varphi(y)$ are comparable in $C_{n,d}$ and so lie in the same block, i.e.\ $\lfloor\varphi(x)/d\rfloor=\lfloor\varphi(y)/d\rfloor$; equal second coordinates give $\varphi(x)\equiv\varphi(y)\pmod d$. Same quotient and remainder force $\varphi(x)=\varphi(y)$, hence $x=y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bonferroni family bound]\label{thm:family}
Let $H$ be a host on $N$ points containing induced copies of $n$-element posets $P_{0},\dots,P_{k-1}$, with $(P_{i},P_{j})$ of common induced bound $s_{ij}$ for $j<i$. Then
\[
k\,n\ \le\ N+\sum_{i<k}\sum_{j<i}s_{ij}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $A_{i}$ be the image of the copy of $P_{i}$, so $|A_{i}|=n$. The Bonferroni inequality $|\bigcup_{i<k}A_{i}|\ge\sum_{i<k}|A_{i}|-\sum_{i<k}\sum_{j<i}|A_{i}\cap A_{j}|$ (induction on $k$) with $|A_{i}\cap A_{j}|\le s_{ij}$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:overlap}) and $|\bigcup A_{i}|\le N$ gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:geomoverlap}
For $j<i<k$ and $n=4^{k}$, the pair $(C_{n,4^{i}},C_{n,4^{j}})$ has common induced bound $4^{\,k-i}4^{\,j}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:chainunion} gives $(\lfloor(4^{k}-1)/4^{i}\rfloor+1)\cdot4^{j}$; since $4^{k}-1<4^{i}\cdot4^{k-i}$ the floor is $<4^{k-i}$, so the first factor is at most $4^{k-i}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:geomsum}
$\displaystyle 3\sum_{i<k}\sum_{j<i}4^{\,k-i}4^{\,j}\ \le\ k\,4^{k}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By induction, $3\sum_{j<i}4^{j}+1=4^{i}$, so $3\sum_{j<i}4^{j}=4^{i}-1\le4^{i}$ and
$3\sum_{i<k}\sum_{j<i}4^{k-i}4^{j}=\sum_{i<k}4^{k-i}(4^{i}-1)\le\sum_{i<k}4^{k}=k4^{k}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Superlinear bound at powers of four]\label{thm:super4}
For every $k$, $\ 2k\,4^{k}\le 3\,U(4^{k})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:family} with $n=4^{k}$, the posets $C_{n,4^{i}}$ for $i<k$, and $s_{ij}=4^{k-i}4^{j}$: $k4^{k}\le U(4^{k})+S$ with $3S\le k4^{k}$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:geomsum}. Multiplying by $3$ gives $3k4^{k}\le 3U(4^{k})+k4^{k}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[General form; superlinearity]\label{thm:superlin}
For every $n$, $\ n\lfloor\log_{4}n\rfloor\le 6\,U(n)$. Consequently $U(n)/n\to\infty$: for every $C$ and every $m$ there is $n\ge m$ with $U(n)\ge Cn$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $k=\lfloor\log_{4}n\rfloor$, so $4^{k}\le n<4\cdot4^{k}$. Monotonicity (Theorem~\ref{thm:mono}) gives $U(4^{k})\le U(n)$, so $2k4^{k}\le3U(n)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:super4}, and
\[
kn\le k\cdot4\cdot4^{k}=2\bigl(2k4^{k}\bigr)\le 6U(n).
\]
For superlinearity take $n=4^{k}$ with $k\ge\max(6C,m)$: then $6Cn\le kn\le6U(n)$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Why the ratio must exceed \texorpdfstring{$2$}{2}}

With block sizes $r^{0},\dots,r^{k-1}$ and $n=r^{k}$, the overlaps sum to at most
\[
\sum_{i<k}r^{k-i}\cdot\frac{r^{i}-1}{r-1}\ \le\ \frac{k\,r^{k}}{r-1},
\]
so Theorem~\ref{thm:family} gives $k r^{k}\bigl(1-\tfrac{1}{r-1}\bigr)\le U(r^{k})$, nontrivial exactly when $r>2$. At $r=2$ the terms cancel and the bound degenerates: the geometric series of overlaps grows precisely as fast as the gain from an extra family member. Ratio $2$ is the threshold of the method; $r=4$ is the smallest integer ratio comfortably above it, with the additional virtue that $3\sum_{j<i}4^{j}+1=4^{i}$ is an exact identity, hiding no rounding.

The bound of Theorem~\ref{thm:superlin} is $\Theta(n\log n)$ and therefore still infinitely far below $2^{(n-1)/4}$; its interest is methodological. It shows that a method with no counting content whatsoever escapes linearity, and it isolates both the reason (the multiplicativity of Lemma~\ref{lem:chainunion}) and the exact threshold at which the escape fails.

\section{The comparability functor, labelling schemes, and regularity}\label{sec:graphs}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{comparability graph} $G(P)$ of a poset $P$ has the same vertices, with $x\sim y$ iff $x\ne y$ and ($x\le y$ or $y\le x$).
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:compbip}
$G(B(R))$ is the bipartite graph on $[k]\sqcup[l]$ whose edges are exactly the pairs $\{a_{i},b_{j}\}$ with $(i,j)\in R$; no two $a$'s and no two $b$'s are adjacent.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Functoriality and the graph counting bound]\label{thm:functor}
If $H$ is a $(k,l)$-bipartite-universal poset then $G(H)$ contains every $(k,l)$-bipartite graph as an induced subgraph. Consequently, if a graph on $N$ vertices contains all $(k,l)$-bipartite graphs as induced subgraphs, then $2^{kl}\le N^{k+l}$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:count} follows.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
An induced poset embedding $f$ is injective, and for $x\ne y$, $f(x)\sim f(y)$ iff $f(x),f(y)$ are comparable iff $x,y$ are comparable iff $x\sim y$. The graph counting bound is proved exactly as Theorem~\ref{thm:count}: adjacency between the parts reconstructs the graph, so distinct bipartite graphs cannot share an embedding.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Regularity for comparability graphs]\label{thm:reg}
For every $\varepsilon>0$ and every $m$ there is $M=M(\varepsilon,m)$ such that every finite poset $P$ with at least $m$ points admits a partition of its points into $t$ parts with $m\le t\le M$, all sizes differing by at most one, which is $\varepsilon$-uniform for $G(P)$: for all but at most $\varepsilon t^{2}$ ordered pairs of parts $(V_{i},V_{j})$, and all $X\subseteq V_{i}$, $Y\subseteq V_{j}$ with $|X|\ge\varepsilon|V_{i}|$, $|Y|\ge\varepsilon|V_{j}|$, one has $|d(X,Y)-d(V_{i},V_{j})|\le\varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply the Szemer\'edi Regularity Lemma to $G(P)$; the bound $M$ is independent of $P$.
\end{proof}

This is the form in which regularity is deployed in the construction of hosts of size $2^{(1+\eta)n/2}$: the host is built as a labelling scheme whose labels record, for each element, its position relative to a bounded regular partition together with a bounded amount of exceptional data, and transitivity of the reconstructed order is guaranteed by the design of the labels rather than checked case by case.

\subsection{Adjacency labelling schemes}

\begin{definition}
An \emph{adjacency labelling scheme} of length $L$ for the $n$-element posets consists of, for every poset $P$ on $n$ points, an injective assignment of $L$-bit labels to its points, and a single decoder $\delta:\{0,1\}^{L}\times\{0,1\}^{L}\to\{0,1\}$ independent of $P$ with $\delta(\ell(x),\ell(y))=1\iff x\le_{P}y$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Correspondence]
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item An induced-universal host $H$ with $N$ points yields a scheme of length $\lceil\log_{2}N\rceil$: label $x$ by the index of $f(x)$ in $H$, with $\delta$ the order table of $H$.
\item Conversely, a scheme of length $L$ yields a set $V$ of at most $2^{L}$ labels carrying the decoder relation $u\preceq v\iff\delta(u,v)=1$, such that every $n$-element poset embeds into $(V,\preceq)$ as an induced substructure. If $\preceq$ is itself a partial order --- as it is for every scheme obtained from a host as in (i) --- then $(V,\preceq)$ is an induced-universal host on at most $2^{L}$ points.
\end{enumerate}
Consequently the optimal length among schemes whose decoder relation is an order is exactly $\lceil\log_{2}U(n)\rceil$.
\end{proposition}

Thus: the Boolean-lattice host is the scheme ``label $x$ by the characteristic vector of $\down x$'', of length $n$; the tagged-neighbourhood host is ``label a top element by its neighbourhood plus its serial number'', of length $k+\lceil\log_{2}l\rceil+O(1)$, i.e.\ $n/2+O(\log n)$ bits on balanced parts; the counting bound says no scheme can have length below $(n-1)/4$; and the $2^{(1+\eta)n/2}$ theorem says length $(1+\eta)n/2$ is achievable for all posets. Such schemes are the mechanism behind succinct reachability structures, distributed ancestry queries in version-control and taxonomy systems, and local decidability of dominance without a global index.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{A. Principal-ideal labelling.} Input: an $n$-element poset as an $n\times n$ Boolean matrix. Output: the $n$-bit label $\down x$ for each $x$. Cost: $O(n^{2})$ time, $n$ bits per label; decoding ``$x\le y$?'' is a subset test. Correctness: Lemma~\ref{lem:ideal}.

\paragraph{B. Tagged-neighbourhood labelling for bipartite posets.} Input: $R\subseteq[k]\times[l]$. Output: $a_{i}\mapsto(\textsf{bot},i)$, $b_{j}\mapsto(\textsf{top},N_{R}(j),j)$. Cost: $O(kl)$ time; label length $k+\lceil\log_{2}l\rceil+1$ bits. Correctness: Theorem~\ref{thm:bipuniv}, Proposition~\ref{prop:tag}.

\paragraph{C. Overlap lower-bound certificate.} Input: a family $P_{0},\dots,P_{k-1}$ of $n$-element posets and a matrix of common induced bounds $s_{ij}$. Output: the certificate $N\ge kn-\sum_{i<k}\sum_{j<i}s_{ij}$. Cost: $O(k^{2})$ once the $s_{ij}$ are known; for chain-unions each $s_{ij}$ is $O(1)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:chainunion}. Correctness: Theorem~\ref{thm:family}.

\paragraph{D. Exhaustive host verification.} Input: a candidate host on $N$ points and the size $n$. Enumerate all $2^{n^{2}}$ relations on $[n]$, filter the partial orders, and search the $N^{n}$ maps for an induced embedding. Prohibitive in general, but for $n\le4$ ($2^{16}$ relations, $219$ orders, $8^{4}$ maps) a few million operations. This certifies $U(3)\le5$ and $U(4)\le8$.

\section{Discussion}

Combining everything, for all $n\ge1$,
\[
\max\Bigl(2n-1,\ 3n-\lceil n/2\rceil-3,\ \tfrac{1}{6}n\log_{4}n,\ 2^{(n-1)/4}\Bigr)\ \le\ U(n)\ \le\ 2^{n}-1,
\]
and the deep theorem narrows the ceiling to $2^{(1+\eta)n/2}$ for large $n$. The exponent of $U(n)$ lies in $[1/4,1/2]$.

\paragraph{Two incomparable methods.} The counting bound is exponential but blind to structure; the overlap bound is structural but, for the families considered, at most $\Theta(n\log n)$. They are complementary: counting gives nothing useful for $n\le20$, overlap gives the exact values at $n\le3$ and the sharp bound $7$ at $n=4$, and counting takes over around $n\approx25$. Neither reaches the truth, and the reasons are well localised: counting cannot see reuse of host points; overlap cannot see that most posets are pairwise highly compatible.

\paragraph{Where the $\eta$ comes from.} The tagged-neighbourhood host shows the exponent $n/2$ is achievable exactly on height-$\le2$ posets, with only $O(\log n)$ additive overhead from the tag. The $\eta$ is therefore not a defect of the bipartite core; it is the price of extending the labelling to unbounded height.

\paragraph{Is $1/2$ the truth?} Three data points argue mildly for the upper end. (i) The counting bound is lossy in every case where the answer is known: $U(2)=3$ against a bound of $2$, $U(3)=5$ against $2^{1/2}$. (ii) The counting method has an intrinsic ceiling of $1/4$, so its failure to reach $1/2$ is not evidence against $1/2$. (iii) The balanced bipartite family, which generates the counting bound, is hosted by an explicit construction of exponent exactly $1/2$, and the neighbourhood field cannot be compressed below $k$ bits without collisions among the $2^{k}$ realisable neighbourhoods. Against this: nobody has produced a lower bound above $1/4$.

\section{Future work}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Decide $U(4)$.} Is there a seven-point induced-universal host for the four-element posets? A finite search with heavy symmetry reduction available; settling it would give the fifth exact value and calibrate which lower-bound method is closer to the truth at small $n$.
\item \textbf{Push the overlap method past $n\log n$.} Lemma~\ref{lem:chainunion} is a product of two pigeonhole constraints. Families whose common induced subposets are bounded by a product of three constraints might give $n\log^{2}n$ or $n^{1+c}$. Is there a family for which the method yields a superpolynomial bound?
\item \textbf{Hybridise counting and overlap.} Partition the $n$-element posets into incompatibility classes and apply the counting bound within each; the class structure would multiply the counting bound by the number of classes.
\item \textbf{Remove the $\eta$.} Is $U(n)=2^{n/2+o(n)}$, or even $O(n^{c}2^{n/2})$? The tagged-neighbourhood host suggests the polynomial-factor version is correct on height-$\le2$ posets.
\item \textbf{Lower bounds above $2^{n/4}$.} Any bound $2^{cn}$ with $c>1/4$ would be the first improvement over pure counting, and must use structure --- plausibly a reuse argument bounding how many embeddings a single host point can serve.
\item \textbf{Height-restricted classes.} Determine the exponent for posets of height at most $h$. Corollary~\ref{cor:sandwich} settles $h=2$ up to $[1/4,1/2]$; is the answer $1/2$ for every fixed $h\ge2$?
\item \textbf{Effective regularity.} Is there a construction of size $2^{(1+\eta)n/2}$ whose host and embeddings are computable in time polynomial in the host size?
\end{enumerate}

\end{document}

